# Outbound MultiCloud Authentication

The OpenStack adapter's MultiCloud client supports optional HTTP Basic
authentication. Configure the application at startup using Spring properties or
their environment-variable equivalents:

| Spring property | Environment variable |
| --- | --- |
| `multicloud.auth.username` | `MULTICLOUD_AUTH_USERNAME` |
| `multicloud.auth.password` | `MULTICLOUD_AUTH_PASSWORD` |

Both values default to empty. If both are absent or empty, including a mixture
of absent and empty, no Authorization header is added and existing unauthenticated
behavior is preserved. To enable authentication, supply both values from the
runtime secret-management mechanism. Values are plaintext credentials, not
encrypted SO passwords or pre-encoded Basic headers. Do not store them in source
control or include them in logs or command-line arguments.

Partial configuration, whitespace-only fields, and a username containing a colon
cause application initialization to fail. The validation error names the properties
without revealing their values. Passwords may contain colons, dollar signs,
quotes, and non-ASCII characters. Credentials are preserved without trimming and
encoded as UTF-8 before Base64 encoding. Both values are opaque literal strings:
Spring placeholders and SpEL expressions are not evaluated, including known
placeholders such as `literal${HOME}`, `literal#{2*3}`, and unmatched expressions.
Supply the credential itself, not a `${MULTICLOUD_AUTH_PASSWORD}` property alias.
Ensure the runtime configuration mechanism
passes special characters literally without shell interpolation. Restart the
application after changing credentials; they are validated and captured at startup.

Authentication applies to every request created by `MsoMulticloudUtils`, including
workload creation, status queries/polling, deletion, and MultiCloud-mediated AAI
updates. The existing Project header and tracing remain in place. Direct AAI
clients and unrelated outbound clients do not receive these credentials. Existing
request and response error handling is unchanged.

## TLS Requirement

Basic authentication only Base64-encodes credentials; it does not encrypt them.
Use a trusted HTTPS endpoint whenever authentication is enabled. Set
`mso.msb-scheme=https` (or `MSB_SCHEME=https`) and configure `mso.msb-ip` and
`mso.msb-port` for the TLS endpoint. An explicit `MSB_SCHEME` environment variable
overrides the property. The existing HTTP default is unchanged, so enabling
authentication does not automatically enable TLS. Verify certificate trust and
protect any proxy-to-MultiCloud hop carrying the Authorization header with TLS
or an equivalently protected transport. Do not disable certificate validation.
